 6.2 For link states $x,y$ with $s$ defects, define $\psi(x,y)$ as the coefficient of $1_s$ in $y\circ x^*$, and set it to zero when this composite has fewer than $s$ propagating components. For link states $z,w$, associativity and the quotient action give $z\cdot(x^*\circ w)=\psi(x,z)w$: when $z\circ x^*$ has fewer than $s$ propagating components, the resulting term is zero in $W_{m,s}$. This pairing is nonzero if $s>0$: for no arcs, pair the all-defect state with itself; with $p=(m-s)/2>0$, pair the states with arcs $(1,2),(3,4),\ldots,(2p-1,2p)$ and defects to their right, and with arcs $(2,3),(4,5),\ldots,(2p,2p+1)$ and a defect at site $1$, respectively. Their overlay has no loop and gives value $1$. If $s=0$, the case needed below has $t=1$ and $q^4\ne1$, hence $\delta\ne0$; pairing any perfect matching with itself gives $\delta^{m/2}\ne0$. Choose $x,y$ with $\psi(x,y)=1$. For every $w$, the element $x^*\circ w$ then satisfies $y\cdot(x^*\circ w)=w$. If $\Theta$ is a module endomorphism, $\Theta(w)=\Theta(y)\cdot(x^*\circ w)=\psi(x,\Theta(y))w$. Thus every module endomorphism of these summands is scalar. [A1, F1, F2, step 5.1, construct, algebra]
 
